Workstation V2.00a, Plus V3.00a (mid-March 90):

1) Repair to "insufficient EMS memory", other EMS memory error messages.

2) Reduction in size of .EXE file by about 30K to help increase the range of
   executable programs that can be used with the new External Module
   Configuration function.


Workstation V2.01, Plus 3.01

1) Complete overhaul and update of on-line HELP file.  Also, enhancements in
   the way HELP display works, including use of left and right mouse keys to
   jump to beginning and end of help display, respectively.

2) Repair to file acquisition with sampling rate entry above 1.xx MHz.

3) Correction to log frequency axis display where some "orders of magnitude"
   values would cause waveform display to extend past left edge of display.

4) Correction to Data Translation 282x series 2-channel D/A playback sampling
   rate.

5) Correction to Digital Audio Labs DRB1 and Data Translation DT282x D/A
   playback.

6) Addition of User Setup field "Zoom Slew Rate" to control rate of "rapid"
   mouse movement in waveform displays.

7) "Keyboard only" selection in Mouse Configuration function now reprograms
   keyboard for 3x faster operation, making waveform display zoom and other
   target window-based functions much easier to use.

8) Repair to graphical filter desired response editing when in "scaled up"
   (PgUp key) display modes.

9) Correction to SINEGEN.DOC example external module file.  Also, a new
   CONFIG.DOC file that is referenced by all Hyperception-provided example
   external modules has been created.  This file performs read/write
   interface to Hypersignal configuration files, and includes extensive
   documentation and comments that describe the structure of data inside
   configuration save files, as well as how to change the data.  Another
   example external module has also been added, "PTRACKER", which is a
   rudimentary pitch estimator and tracker for use in vocal work.


Workstation V2.02, Plus 3.02

CORRECTIONS, REPAIRS
--------------------

1)  Repair to Differentiator FIR filter design, where the program initialized
    the entire differentiator response (usually a single "transition band-
    width") to "undefined", and no desired response showed in the initial
    filter design graphical display.

2)  Correction to PC-56 and DSP-C25 board sampling rate and ADC filter
    programming.  Also, AIC chip control word is now available in the config-
    uration file for these boards, so that default gain, loopback, and ADC
    filter enable/disable settings can be easily changed.

3)  Repair to Texas Instruments TMS320C25 SWDS board analog functions (scope,
    analyzer, etc.) which were not working.

4)  Repair to program operation with monochrome (e.g. Hercules) displays.

5)  Repair to entry in Graphics Configuration menu, which was only accepting
    values of 70 or less.

6)  Repair to File Acquisition problem where "Channel Strip" values greater
    than zero caused the software to treat the output file as multichannel.
    In the process, a new "Post-Acquisition" field has been added to the File
    Acquisition function allowing Framesize, FFT Order, and Number of Channels
    to be specified for output file header entry, to take effect in subsequent
    processing after the File Acquisition function.

7)  All frequency domain display functions, including the Spectrum Analyzer,
    have been updated to take into account final-output scaling caused by use
    of any non-rectangular time domain window prior to the FFT.

8)  Repair to on-line HELP display from Analogic AT6400, Data Translation
    DT282x, or Digital Audio Labs DRB1 Spectrum Analyzer functions that may
    have crashed due to stack misallocation.

9)  Correction to real/imag waveform data files, which had (# of frames)*2
    extra points being written out.

10) Correction to Convolution/Correlation function when the h[n] file exceeds
    16384 points in total length.

11) Corrections to TMS320C30 filter code generation, including basic design
    flaw in IIR structure 2 and mislabeling of IIR structure 1 and 2
    coefficient storage (coefficients were stored correctly, but A1i, A2i
    labels were reversed) and improvements to FIR code and comments.

12) Device driver added for AT&T DSP32C development board T7520 codec.
    'ATT32C' selection from System Configuration should now work correctly
    for analog-related functions.  Also, codec control word is now available
    in configuration file (see variable "ATT_CTRL_WORD" in "CONFIG.DOC" file)
    should Hypersignal default settings of codec need to be altered.

13) Corrections made to D/A playback for CAC DSP32C-AT board.

14) Repair to "mean subtraction" in File Acquisition function, which was not
    working.

15) Correction to structure 1 and 2 IIR filter code generator for Motorola
    DSP56000, where "intermediate stage scaling" statements that should
    be "commented" for optional use only were not.

16) Repair to 3-D Spectrogram plotting with log-frequency axes and the "mesh"
    option ("surface" field set to 'M') both enabled.

17) Repair to cases of Data Translation 282x board lock-up with certain,
    infrequently occurring sampling rate values. This appears to be a hardware
    problem, where the boards fail to operate with a "counter" value of 0FFH.

18) Repair to labeling of Spectrum Analyzer lefthand edge of frequency axis
    (log mode only), which was displaying incorrect value.

19) Repair to Power Spectra Generation function which occasionally coughed up
    a run-time error for some combinations of FFT parameters followed by a
    straight section-average power spectra generation.

20) Repair to built-in F2 print function in following case:  EMS memory print
    from 2-D Spectrograph function with current screen resolution 640x480 or
    below (for VGA/EGA cards) or 720x350 or below (Hercules mono-type cards).
    Symptom was that box descriptors and units did show up on printout, and
    then disappeared from screen also.  Screen dumps (non-EMS) were okay and
    were unaffected.

21) Same problem in 20) above could've caused color printer (e.g. HP PaintJet)
    printouts to have either no box descriptors and units or garbled ones.
    Color printout text should now be okay regardless of current screen
    resolution.

22) Correction to DSP56001 Spectrum Analyzer log magnitude display, which
    was adding an erroneous 6 dB to all signals (i.e. for a 16-bit A/D input,
    a sine wave would appear to be full-scale, or 0 dB, when the peak input A/D
    value was only 16422).

23) Correction to Spectrum Analyzer dual display from file, which was crashing
    in some cases.

24) Correction to unity-gain problem in convolution with FIR Filter
    Construction-generated output when filter coefficient(s) exceeded 1.00.

25) Correction to Hercules graphics digital scope and spectrum analyzer
    display where page 2 had some uninitialized garbage.

26) Correction to impulse responses generated for IIR filters where first
    sample was zero, causing extra pi shift in phase response.

27) Correction to File Acquisition function import of 8-bit unsigned data.

28) Correction to Stimulus & Response operation where this operation had to be
    the first performed inside the Analog Conversion function in order to
    work correctly.

29) Correction to DT282x board Spectrum Analyzer voltage scaling based on
    entry in INPUT RANGE field.


ENHANCEMENTS, NEW FEATURES
--------------------------

1)  Significant key commands that may not easily be remembered inside waveform
    display functions are now listed in a column to the right of displays.

2)  Enabling of PgUp/PgDn scaling while in linear amplitude mode in filter
    design display.

3)  Improved scaling and calibration scheme for log displays in Spectrum
    Analyzer.  See on-line HELP for explanation.

4)  D/A playback from time Waveform Display/Edit capable of higher rates in
    general, due to increased buffer sizes.

5)  Removal of maximum length on cut-and-paste editing operations.  Any size
    chunk of waveform data may now be inserted/deleted/copied.

6)  Improvement to estimation of length for FIR Kaiser-Window and Parks-
    McClellan filters.

7)  Addition of "zoom-undo" function that allows "backing-out" from zoomed
    waveform displays.  A "zoom-history" is kept up to 16 levels; the two
    outer keys on the mouse are pressed simultaneously to back up to the
    previous zoom level.

8)  Further addition of automatic "stereo/analytic sampling" waveform
    handling to time domain functions and stereo waveform handling to the
    Power Spectra Generation frequency domain function.  These functions
    handle stereo waveforms and complex waveforms automatically, without
    manual intervention, creating stereo or complex output for further
    processing or display.

9)  Addition of "rubber-band" mode to time domain waveform editor.  To
    accommodate "region-mode" and "point-mode" commands that allow switching
    between cut-and-paste and rubber-band editor modes, the "P" key is now
    used to enter point-mode (as it always has been in graphical filter
    design); the "B" key is now used for controlling the display background
    color (used to be the "P" key), and the Backspace key pans frame displays
    backwards (since the space bar pans forwards).  The Backspace key (instead
    of "B" key) change affects ALL program graphical displays.

    Once in point-mode, use the 'Home' key (middle button on mouse) to "tack"
    and "untack" the waveform to the crosshair cursor.  When the waveform is
    attached to the cursor, you can freely move waveform points around.

10) Copy and overwrite options added to cut-and-paste mode editing.  Pressing
    the 'DEL' key causes a prompt asking for either a delete ('D') or copy
    ('C') function (instead of the old 'Y/N' prompt).  Pressing the 'INS' key
    causes a prompt asking for either an insert ('I') or overwrite ('W')
    function.  Below is a summary of what happens with the four basic
    cut-and-paste mode editing functions:

    -Delete:  data inside target (zoom) window read into internal "paste"
              buffer, then deleted from waveform

    -Copy:    data inside target (zoom) window read into internal "paste"
              buffer, waveform is unchanged

    -Insert:  data currently inside internal "paste" buffer inserted into
              waveform starting at left edge of target window (i.e. current
              window width irrelevant)

    -Write:   data currently inside internal "paste" buffer overwritten onto
              waveform starting at left edge of target window (i.e. current
              window width irrelevant)

11) All instrumentation functions now write non-screen-scaled data to disk
    when the "WRITE" function is invoked.  The data written is exactly as it
    was received from the DSP/analog board.  Due to this change, all DSP
    source code had to be completely overhauled.  We took advantage of the
    opportunity to clean many things up, greatly increase the modularity of
    the DSP source code, improve the function library documentation and
    more clearly define each function, and improve the documentation and
    procedure for user-modification of the DSP source code.  In general, the
    source is faster, requires less program memory space, and has become easier
    to work with.

    As another side-benefit to this project, the instrumentation displays no
    longer require long pauses when displaying disk file input, and all
    Spectrum Analyzer inter- and intrachannel display combinations are defined
    except for intrachannel (same-channel) display of log power spectra and
    linear power spectra.

12) "Snap-in" real-time filtering for all analog-related functions, either
    FIR or IIR (structures 2 and 3) using either Hypersignal-created or
    user-designed filter coefficient files.  For digital oscilloscope and
    spectrum analyzer enter the filter filename after the "An" input channel
    entries.  For analog conversion (and playback from displays) enter the
    filter filename after the CHANNEL LIST field entry, e.g. '0,1,filtname'.
    Use a comma (',') as a separator before the filter filename.  As an option,
    you may enter the number of bits to quantize filter coefficients to after
    the filter name (again, use a comma as separator).  Otherwise, the program
    will automatically choose the maximum possible quantization level (quanti-
    zation applies only to fixed-point DSPs).
    
13) Installation in Analog Conversion function of "stimulus and response"
    simultaneous D/A playback of waveform disk file during A/D record (using
    optional positive or negative trigger delay).  To specify the stimulus
    (output) file, enter a comma (or other separator) followed by the stimulus
    filename (with no extension) after the 'A' or 'AM' entry in the ACQ/GEN
    field.  To specify a record delay, enter in units of seconds, after 
    "mode" and "level" in the "Trigger" field.  A positive delay will cause
    recording to start at delay seconds AFTER the stimulus has begun; a
    negative delay will cause recording to start delay seconds BEFORE the
    stimulus begins.

14) As the Analog Conversion function is getting more sophisticated, we felt
    that it deserved setup "save" files, as is done with Difference Equations
    and Filter Construction.  These files have ".ADC" extensions, and use the
    same filename as the waveform being recorded or generated.  These setup
    save files should go a long ways in reducing confusion between separate
    Analog Conversion operations that use different gain settings, channel
    lists, etc.  Also, these files will make it more difficult to accidentally
    overwrite important existing waveforms when using the Analog Conversion
    function; thus, we have placed the "dive, dive" audible warning under
    global User Setup sound control.

15) Buffer sizes used by analog functions reduced to roughly 1 buffer/sec
    when sampling rates are less than the maximum buffer size allowed by the
    current analog hardware.  This provides greater resolution in acquiring
    the requested amount of data in low sampling rate operations, and will
    eliminate time spent waiting for huge buffers to fill with extra, unneeded
    data in low sampling rate operations.

16) Acceleration of the Convolution function when using some DSP hardware
    (in general:  TMS320C25, TMS320C30, DSP56001, and DSP32C).  Also, the board
    in use needs at least 24 Kw of free RAM (not including whatever space is
    occupied by executable code).  This means that many boards will need to
    have their expanded-memory option in place.  Hypersignal automatically
    looks for the required memory (usually depends on filter/impulse response
    size); if not found, the user is given the option of continuing using
    standard 80x86/80x87 processing.

17) LSI (Spectrum Signal Proc.) TMS320C30 board support added.  Use 'LSI30'
    or 'LSIC30' in System Configuration menu.

18) Support for PCI200041C carrier board added.  Analog A/D modules 19M, 23M,
    and 341M, D/A module 3M, and the 31M expander/sequencer module (and
    combinations thereof) are currently supported.  Analog Conversion to/from
    disk, Digital Oscilloscope, and D/A playback from from waveform displays
    are active.  Also, with an inexpensive "DSP Coprocessor" board added (such
    as the Burr-Brown ZPB32), the Spectrum Analyzer function can be activated.
    Use 'PCI41C'in the System Configuration menu.

19) Support for the PCI20202C "smart" carrier has been substantially upgraded.
    All TMS320C25 corrections, new features, source code improvements, etc.
    described elsewhere apply also to this board.  Also, specifically for this
    system, two-channel D/A operation has been enabled, and hard disk transfer
    rates have been substantially increased by dynamically locating sections
    of TMS320C25 code into onchip RAM during operation, allowing the TMS320C25
    to continue processing while its external memory is accessed by the host.

20) ASPI (Atlanta Signal Proc.) "Banshee" TMS320C30 board support added.  Use
    'ASPI30' or 'ASPIC30' in System Configuration menu.  Please note than an
    "AD16" daughterboard is required for analog I/O.

21) Sonitech "Spirit-30" TMS320C30 board support added. Use 'SON30' or 'SONC30'
    in System Configuration menu.  Please note that an "IC-100" external unit
    is required for analog I/O.

22) "Monitor" function (listen before, during record) activated for all DSP32C
    and TMS320C25 boards.

23) NEW! NEW! New version 3.6A MetaWindow graphics driver can be loaded into
    "high" memory as a device driver, freeing about 113 kbyte of DOS memory for
    User-Defined external programs (or other, uses, such as memory-resident
    utilities).  Use the "LOADHI" command in your EMS memory manager to load
    the met_shel.exe program into high DOS (above 640K but below 1024K) memory.

24) Addition of more 1024x768 graphics hardware modes.

25) Built-in print function ('F2' key) now prints target window and its labels
    if present on display during print function.

26) When convolution/correlation is now invoked with an arbitrary h[n] file
    (NOT a filter file), a version of Parseval's theorem has been installed to
    ensure unity-power (NOT unity-gain) in the output waveform w.r.t the
    input waveform.  The idea here is that an arbitrary h[n] might increase
    gain at certain frequencies at the expense of other frequencies, but the
    overall power of the output waveform cannot increase beyond that of the
    original input waveform.

27) Texas Instruments EVM TMS320C30 board support added.  Use 'EVM30' or
    'EVMC30' in System Configuration menu.

28) Trace 1 / Trace 2 selector activated in Waveform Display/Edit and 2-D
    Spectrograph functions.  Current active trace affects both waveform
    editing and D/A playback.

29) Transfer Function Generation added to Frequency Domain menu.  Ratio of
    two real/imaginary (complex) waveforms is calculated.  New real/imag
    output, as well as magnitude waveform suitable for 3-D or 2-D viewing,
    is generated.

30) Convolution and stimulus & response have been activated for all
    TMS320C25 boards.  Also, all TMS320C25 DSP Source Code Interface libraries,
    modules were converted to T.I. COFF format.

31) Complete DSP32C upgrade for all DSP32C boards supported.  DSP32C source
    completely rewritten to comply with standardized, modular conventions used
    by other supported DSPs.   All DSP32C boards were given full functionality,
    including snap-in real-time filtering, convolution acceleration,
    stimulus & response, 4096 pt. FFTs, etc.

32) Several Spectrum Analyzer improvements made, including:  addition of 1/N
    Octave Band display (separate option from log-axis display), entry of
    minimum frequency to display for log-axis or octave band display enabled,
    entry of 'NN' frame averaging during transfer function display enabled.

33) ASPI Banshee board performance dramatically upgraded by using new
    TMS320C30 DMA controller data-transfer technique.

34) An external clock option can now be "attached" to each entry in the
    System Configuration menu for boards that support such a feature.

35) A "headitor" (header-editor) has been written in the form of the external
    module "HEADITOR.EXE" that can be used to directly edit any part of a
    Hypersignal waveform header.

36) Support added for Burr-Brown ZPB32 and ZPB34 boards, including the
    following analog I/O modules:  ZPD 1001, ZPD 1002, ZPB1100, ZPB2100,
    and ZPB6064 interface board.  The ZPD 1002 allows 10 MHz Spectrum
    Analyzer and Digital Oscilloscope operation.

37) A new Software Reference Guide & Tutorial has been published and is
    shipping with all V2.02s.  The SRG contains about a dozen practical
    DSP examples, including sampling rate conversion, heterodyning,
    Hilbert Transform design, waveform editing, etc.

38) A new Hardware Reference Guide has been published and is shipping.  The
    HRG contains individual sections on each supported board, with very
    specific information and details about the board and its use in
    conjunction with Hypersignal.  Memory usage (and memory expansion
    recommendations), as well as significant deviations from standard
    Hypersignal operation, if any, are discussed.

    Also, the HRG now contains all documentation concerning the DSP Source
    Code Interface, which now includes 7 different DSP chips.  This section
    has been greatly upgraded and better organized; the standardization
    in code modification and testing across DSP chip families that we have
    been striving for is now paying dividends.


Workstation V2.03, Plus 3.03

CORRECTIONS, REPAIRS
--------------------

1)  Following transfer function bugs fixed:  crash with 4096 pt. FFT size,
    misalignment of multiple frame output (which caused invalid 2-D and 3-D
    Spectrograph viewing). 

2)  Fixed obscure problem in Convolution when samples beyond "FRAMESIZE" in
    H[n] were mistakenly used in the H[n] array.

3)  Selecting trace 2 in Waveform Display/Edit and 2-D Spectrograph now also
    correctly selects trace 2 sampling rate (if different) for D/A playback.
    Also, Waveform Display/Edit now changes time axis and zoom box units
    readout based on new sampling frequency.

4)  Incorrect wait-state register value for LSI DSP32C board in "reset.s"
    source module of "DSP32.OUT" file fixed (correct value is 2).

5)  Problem where Magnitude Display, Phase Display, and 3-D Spectrogram were
    displaying 1 too many samples in each frame fixed (problem only occurred
    when original time domain data was complex).

6)  Analog Conversion, Digital Oscilloscope, and Spectrum Analyzer functions
    changed to have no limit on number of channels entered in menu CHANNEL
    LIST field (possible entry now is from 0 to 63), regardless of the DSP
    board in use (only applies to hardware with onboard DSP chip).

7)  Fix to data-entry menu background color for some monochrome displays,
    where data-entry menu descriptions and input text could not be seen.

8)  Polarity corrected for write to reset port within general-purpose DSP56001
    host-interface (applies only to Hyperception-manufactured "Hyper-ADM"
    board).  Also, post reset-pulse delay increased to 100 msec.

9)  Relative amplitude problem fixed in frequency-domain Magnitude Display and
    3-D Spectrogram Display log display when "Dual Autoscaling" is enabled.

10) Difference Equation bug where one extra output sample was calculated and
    used to determine maximum output amplitude (but not actually written to
    output file) has been corrected.

11) Fixed problem with DSP56001 FIR filter code generation where second 
    half of coefficients were being written out inverted.  This problem
    was accidentally introduced into version 2.02.

12) Fixed long-term problem where .BAT (batch) files would not execute from
    external module menu selections.

13) Fixed problem where screen dumps (not EMS memory re-draws) from 2-D
    Spectrograph function would not show gray-scale on B/W printers.

14) Fixed problem where stimulus files with length less than 1280 bytes were
    not being accepted in the Analog Conversion function.

15) Fixed problem where Convolution/Correlation was not estimating required
    amount of DSP board memory correctly for DSP32C boards.

16) Correction to TMS320C25 board Convolution/Correlation acceleration for
    FIR filters with coefficients greater than 1.


ENHANCEMENTS, NEW FEATURES
--------------------------

1)  Final support added for NEC DDK77220 development board.  Complete 77220
    DSP Source Code Interface is now in place.

2)  Support added for Techron TEF20HI system. Special version of "DSP56000.LOD"
    file is required for this system.

